{
  "title": "🔐 Trigonometric Identities — Equations True for ALL Angles",
  "hook": "sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. Always. No matter what θ is — 17°, 42.3°, or even π/7 radians. These 'identities' are universal truths, and they're the key to solving complex trig expressions.",
  "explanation": "<p><b>The Three Fundamental Identities:</b></p><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Identity 1 (Pythagorean)</div><strong>sin²θ + cos²θ = 1</strong></div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Identity 2 (divide Identity 1 by cos²θ)</div><strong>1 + tan²θ = sec²θ</strong></div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">Identity 3 (divide Identity 1 by sin²θ)</div><strong>1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ</strong></div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">From Identity 1</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">Rearrangement</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Useful to eliminate sin</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Useful to eliminate cos</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">tan²θ = sec²θ − 1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Useful in sec-tan problems</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">cot²θ = cosec²θ − 1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">Useful in cosec-cot problems</td></tr></table><p><b>Strategy for proving identities:</b></p><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#7B1FA2;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Start from one side (usually the more complex one)</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\"></span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#7B1FA2;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Convert everything to sin and cos</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">tan = sin/cos, sec = 1/cos, etc.</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#7B1FA2;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Simplify using algebra</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Factor, combine fractions, use a²−b² = (a+b)(a−b)</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#7B1FA2;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Reach the other side</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">If stuck, try working from BOTH sides toward the middle</span></div></div></div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Prove:</b> (1 + tan²θ)/(1 + cot²θ) = tan²θ.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Use identities on top and bottom</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">Top: 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ. Bottom: 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ.</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Simplify</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">sec²θ / cosec²θ = (1/cos²θ) / (1/sin²θ)</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong></strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">= sin²θ / cos²θ = tan²θ ∎</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Prove: (sin θ − cos θ)² + (sin θ + cos θ)² = 2.",
    "hint": "Expand both squares and use sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.",
    "answer": "LHS = (sin²θ − 2sinθcosθ + cos²θ) + (sin²θ + 2sinθcosθ + cos²θ) = 1 − 2sinθcosθ + 1 + 2sinθcosθ = 2. ∎"
  },
  "fun_fact": "The identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 is actually the Pythagoras theorem in disguise — in a unit circle (radius 1), the x-coordinate is cos θ and y-coordinate is sin θ, so x² + y² = 1!"
